"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (gravitational wave astronomy). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e. The physics behind it: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (Einstein’s theory of gravity). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> GW150914: two black holes; Equal masses; Heavy pair, lower pitch; GW170817: two neutron stars (these use the Inspiral view); Arms stretching, How small? The peak, A wave the arms miss and Livingston first, {#} ms (the Detector view); The real signal and Weigh the black holes (the GW150914 data view); Gravity against light and If gravity were slower (the Race with light view). This worksheet uses <strong>GW150914</strong>, <strong>Equal masses</strong>, <strong>Heavy pair, lower pitch</strong>, <strong>How small? The peak</strong>, <strong>Gravity against light</strong> and <strong>If gravity were slower</strong>.",
"The Inspiral view shows two black holes orbiting each other, seen from above, and below it the wave they send out: its height is the strain h and its frequency climbs as the orbit shrinks. Play runs it in slow motion (the speed is shown on the picture).",
