"Measure how the pitch of two merging black holes depends on their mass, find how much mass turns into gravitational waves, and see how small and how fast those waves are when they reach Earth.",
"<strong>Ripples in spacetime.</strong> In Einstein’s gravity, mass curves spacetime. Masses that swing around each other quickly send out ripples in that curvature, <strong>gravitational waves</strong>, which travel through space at the speed of light. A wave passing through stretches distances one way and squeezes them the other way by a tiny fraction called the <strong>strain</strong> h: a length L changes by",
"\\[\\Delta L = hL.\\]",
"The LIGO detectors in Hanford, Washington and Livingston, Louisiana have arms {#} m long (about {#} km) and can measure a change far smaller than a proton, whose diameter is about \\(1.68\\times10^{-15}\\) m.",
"<strong>The chirp.</strong> Two black holes in orbit lose energy to the waves they send out, so they spiral closer together and orbit faster. The waves rise in frequency (twice the number of orbits per second) and in strength, a rising “chirp”. The spiral ends at the <strong>last stable orbit</strong>, the closest orbit that can hold; inside it the two plunge together and merge. The new black hole then shakes off its lumps and “rings down” like a struck bell at its <strong>ringdown frequency</strong>.",
"<strong>Bigger is lower.</strong> A black hole’s size grows in proportion to its mass M, so the last stable orbit of a heavier pair is wider and slower. Both the last stable orbit frequency and the ringdown frequency go as \\(1/M\\): twice the mass, half the frequency. Big bells ring low, too.",
"<strong>Mass into waves.</strong> The final black hole has less mass than the two that went in. The difference \\(\\Delta m\\) left as gravitational wave energy, \\(E = \\Delta m\\,c^2\\).",
"<strong>The first detection.</strong> On {#} September {#} both LIGO detectors caught GW150914, two black holes of about {#} and {#} solar masses about {#} billion light years away. On {#} August {#} they caught two neutron stars merging (GW170817), and {#} seconds later space telescopes caught a burst of gamma rays from the same spot, {#} million light years away.",
"The frequencies in the demo are the ones measured on Earth: the waves crossed an expanding universe, which stretched them by about {#}%. That changes every frequency by the same factor and does not affect the comparisons in this lab.",
"\\[c = 3.00\\times10^8\\ \\text{m/s},\\quad M_\\odot = 1.988\\times10^{30}\\ \\text{kg}.\\]",
"GW150914 changed the strain at Hanford by about \\(h = 1.0\\times10^{-21}\\). By how much did a {#} m arm change length? What fraction of a proton’s diameter is that?",
"Suppose gravity traveled {#}% slower than light. The waves from GW150914 traveled for {#} billion years. How much later than light would they have arrived?",
